The character was originally a verb meaning “to meet”, whose modern pronunciation is ''yàn''. It was later borrowed for the [[proximal]] [[demonstrative]] “this/here”, by a confusion in medieval handwriting with {{zh-l|遮|this}}.

===Etymology 1===
{{zh-forms|s=这|alt=遮-archaic,者-archaic,只-archaic,拓-archaic,J-nonstandard:zhèi,迓-Hakka:liá; yá,邇-Hakka:liá; yá,即-Hokkien:chit,此-Hokkien:chit}}
First attested in the [[Tang]] dynasty; etymology unclear. Proposed etymologies:
* From {{och-l|者|one which}} ({{zh-ref|Lü, 1985}}). {{zh-ref|Wang (1990)}} notes that {{zh-l|*者}} always occurs after what is referred to, whereas {{lang|zh|這}} always before.
*: {{och-l|者|one which}} > {{ltc-l|者|this (''possessive case'')}} > {{ltc-l|者|this (''general demonstrative'')}} > {{cog|cmn|這|tr=zhè}}.
* From {{och-l|之|this; it}} ({{zh-ref|Wang, 1990}}). {{zh-ref|Norman (1988)}} notes that this derivation would be phonologically problematic.

